            <ArticleTitle>Sudden coronary death due to IgG4-related disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>505-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.carpath.2013.05.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1054-8807(13)00138-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 54-year-old male entered the emergency room in cardiorespiratory arrest after syncope at home. Resuscitation was attempted, but the patient died a few hours later. At necropsy, aneurysms were found at the right and left anterior descending coronary arteries. At microscopic examination, there was no significant coronary atherosclerosis, and a dense inflammatory infiltrate was detected, with a high number of igG4-positive cells (94.0 positive cells/hpf). The case illustrates that IgG4-related disease can cause coronary disease and sudden cardiac death.</AbstractText>
